            <ArticleTitle>A Posterior Circulation Stroke Presenting with Isolated Truncal Ataxia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e1709</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7759/cureus.1709</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Vertebral artery dissection is an infrequent and often misdiagnosed cause of stroke. In this case report, we describe a patient with a posterior circulation stroke caused by a vertebral artery dissection, who presented to the emergency department with isolated truncal ataxia. This case emphasizes the importance of obtaining a thorough history and physical exam for all neurologic complaints, including a careful ambulation assessment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
